-- title:
--   First prolongation integers are the localised modular ring
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$. Let `data` consist of a monic $\Phi\in\mathbb Z[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair of $q$-expansions $(j,\,j_{q})$, let `hKr` be the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \bmod q=(C(X)^{q}-X)(C(X)-X^{q})$, and let `hα`, `hβ` assert that the two Hecke homomorphisms $\bar\alpha$, $\bar\beta$ at level $1$ and prime $q$ are integral. Let $P$ be a `PlaceSpecialization` for these data and $R$ a `LevelOneProlongationPair` for $P$; in particular $R$ supplies two regular prolongations $R_1,R_2$ of $A$ to the base change $F=\overline{\mathbb Q}\cdot F^{\mathrm{full}}_{1\cdot q}$ of the full modular function field of level $1\cdot q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}((\mathfrak q))$, residue data towards $k$, Fricke compatibility of $R_2$ with $R_1$, and a clause comparing the residue of $R_1$ with the reduction homomorphism on the localised modular ring. The assertion is that for every $g\in F$ one has $g\in R_1.\mathrm{integers}$ if and only if the $\mathfrak q$-expansion of $g$ lies in `CharPReduction.modularLocalized`$(1\cdot q)$ for $A$ and $\mathrm{red}$, that is, in the localisation of the subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((\mathfrak q))$ generated by the constants from $A$ together with $j(\mathfrak q)$ and $j(\mathfrak q^{q})$ at the complement of the prime ideal of elements whose coefficientwise reduction vanishes.
--
--   This identifies the valuation ring of the first regular prolongation in a level-one prolongation pair with the explicit localised ring of $\mathfrak q$-expansions furnished by the characteristic-$q$ reduction apparatus, so that membership and residues at the first prolongation can be computed from $j(\mathfrak q)$, $j(\mathfrak q^{q})$ and $A$-coefficients. It is the dictionary used throughout the subsequent analysis of the reduction of $X_0(q)$, and is cited by the multiplicative-covering computations of charts, Hasse exponents and intersection data.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_LevelOneProlongationPair_mem_integersFst_iff_coe_mem_modularLocalized.lean

